### `mazemail contacts`

Find contacts.

Finds people you have exchanged mail with by name or address.

Permission: `read`

```sh
mazemail contacts [OPTIONS] <QUERY>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<QUERY>` | Part of a name or address. |
| `--limit <LIMIT>` | Maximum number of results. Default: `20`. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
[
  {
    email: string,  // Address.
    name?: string | null,  // Display name.
  }
]
```

Examples:

```sh
# Find Anna's address
mazemail contacts anna
```

### `mazemail identities`

List your sending identities.

Lists the identities (From addresses) of the accounts in scope. `draft create` picks one automatically when you don't pass --identity.

Permission: `read`

```sh
mazemail identities [OPTIONS]
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--account <ACCOUNT>` | Only this account's identities. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
[
  {
    account: string,  // Account id.
    default: boolean,  // The account's default identity.
    email: string,  // Address.
    id: string,  // Identity id.
    name: string,  // Display name.
  }
]
```

Examples:

```sh
# Show identities
mazemail identities
```

### `mazemail attachment save`

Save an attachment to a file.

Writes the attachment into --to (default: the current directory) under its sanitized file name, never overwriting an existing file.

Permission: `read`

```sh
mazemail attachment save [OPTIONS] <ID>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<ID>` | The attachment's id (from `mazemail show`). |
| `--to <DIR>` | Directory to save into (default: the current directory). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  content_type: string,  // MIME type.
  id: string,  // Attachment id.
  path: string,  // Where the file was written.
  size: integer,  // Size in bytes.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Save into the current directory
mazemail attachment save m_8f3a2c.2 --to .
```

### `mazemail draft create`

Write a new draft, reply or forward.

Creates a draft in Maze for you to review and send. Drafts are harmless: nothing is sent. The identity is picked automatically from the recipients and the replied-to message unless you pass --identity.

Permission: `draft`

```sh
mazemail draft create [OPTIONS]
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--reply <ID>` | Reply to the sender of this message (`m_…`). |
| `--reply-all <ID>` | Reply to the sender and all recipients of this message (`m_…`). |
| `--forward <ID>` | Forward this message (`m_…`). |
| `--identity <IDENTITY>` | Identity to write from; picked automatically from the recipients and the replied-to message when omitted. |
| `--to <ADDRESS>...` | Recipient (repeatable). |
| `--cc <ADDRESS>...` | Cc recipient (repeatable). |
| `--bcc <ADDRESS>...` | Bcc recipient (repeatable). |
| `--subject <SUBJECT>` | Subject (default for replies: "Re: …"). |
| `--body-file <FILE>` | Read the body (Markdown) from a file, `-` for stdin. |
| `--body <BODY>` | The body (Markdown). |
| `--attach <FILE>...` | Attach a file (repeatable). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  id: string,  // Draft id.
  identity?: string | null,  // The identity it is written from.
  subject: string,  // Subject.
  to: string[],  // Recipients.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Reply; identity picked automatically
mazemail draft create --reply m_8f3a2c --body-file reply.md
# New message with the body from stdin
echo 'See you then.' | mazemail draft create --to anna@example.com --subject Friday --body-file -
```

### `mazemail draft edit`

Change a draft.

Replaces the given fields of a draft; everything else stays as it is.

Permission: `draft`

```sh
mazemail draft edit [OPTIONS] <ID>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<ID>` | The draft's id (`d_…`). |
| `--identity <IDENTITY>` | Identity to write from. |
| `--to <ADDRESS>...` | Replace the recipients (repeatable). |
| `--cc <ADDRESS>...` | Replace the Cc recipients (repeatable). |
| `--bcc <ADDRESS>...` | Replace the Bcc recipients (repeatable). |
| `--subject <SUBJECT>` | Replace the subject. |
| `--body-file <FILE>` | Replace the body with a file's content (Markdown), `-` for stdin. |
| `--body <BODY>` | Replace the body (Markdown). |
| `--attach <FILE>...` | Add an attachment (repeatable). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  id: string,  // Draft id.
  identity?: string | null,  // The identity it is written from.
  subject: string,  // Subject.
  to: string[],  // Recipients.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Replace subject and body
mazemail draft edit d_42 --subject 'Re: Friday' --body-file reply.md
```

### `mazemail archive`

Archive messages or threads.

Under the triage profile this becomes a proposal that you accept or reject in Maze's review UI; under the write profile it runs directly.

Permission: `propose`

```sh
mazemail archive <ID>...
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<ID>...` | Message (`m_…`) or thread (`t_…`) ids. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  affected: string[],  // Message ids affected.
  outcome: "applied" | "proposed",  // Applied directly, or a proposal.
  proposal?: string | null,  // The proposal's id, if proposed.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Archive old newsletters (a proposal under triage)
mazemail archive $(mazemail search 'list:news older_than:7d' --ids)
```

### `mazemail move`

Move messages or threads to a folder.

Under the triage profile this becomes a proposal that you accept or reject in Maze's review UI; under the write profile it runs directly.

Permission: `propose`

```sh
mazemail move --to <FOLDER> <ID>...
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--to <FOLDER>` | Target folder id (`f_…`, from `mazemail folders`). Required. |
| `<ID>...` | Message (`m_…`) or thread (`t_…`) ids. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  affected: string[],  // Message ids affected.
  outcome: "applied" | "proposed",  // Applied directly, or a proposal.
  proposal?: string | null,  // The proposal's id, if proposed.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Move to a folder
mazemail move --to f_k2 m_8f3a2c t_19bd
```

### `mazemail tag`

Add or remove tags.

Under the triage profile this becomes a proposal that you accept or reject in Maze's review UI; under the write profile it runs directly.

Permission: `propose`

```sh
mazemail tag [OPTIONS] <ID>...
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--add <TAG>...` | Tag to add (repeatable). |
| `--remove <TAG>...` | Tag to remove (repeatable). |
| `<ID>...` | Message (`m_…`) or thread (`t_…`) ids. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  affected: string[],  // Message ids affected.
  outcome: "applied" | "proposed",  // Applied directly, or a proposal.
  proposal?: string | null,  // The proposal's id, if proposed.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Retag a message
mazemail tag --add 'To Do' --remove Later m_8f3a2c
```

### `mazemail star`

Star or unstar messages.

Under the triage profile this becomes a proposal that you accept or reject in Maze's review UI; under the write profile it runs directly.

Permission: `propose`

```sh
mazemail star [OPTIONS] <ID>...
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--unstar` | Remove the star instead. |
| `<ID>...` | Message (`m_…`) or thread (`t_…`) ids. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  affected: string[],  // Message ids affected.
  outcome: "applied" | "proposed",  // Applied directly, or a proposal.
  proposal?: string | null,  // The proposal's id, if proposed.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Star a message
mazemail star m_8f3a2c
# Unstar a thread
mazemail star --unstar t_19bd
```

### `mazemail read`

Mark messages read or unread.

Under the triage profile this becomes a proposal that you accept or reject in Maze's review UI; under the write profile it runs directly.

Permission: `propose`

```sh
mazemail read [OPTIONS] <ID>...
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--unread` | Mark as unread instead. |
| `<ID>...` | Message (`m_…`) or thread (`t_…`) ids. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  affected: string[],  // Message ids affected.
  outcome: "applied" | "proposed",  // Applied directly, or a proposal.
  proposal?: string | null,  // The proposal's id, if proposed.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Mark a thread unread
mazemail read --unread t_19bd
```

### `mazemail propose`

Propose a batch of actions for review.

Sends a list of actions (archive, move, tag, star, mark_read, unsubscribe) that shows up grouped in Maze's review UI, where you accept or reject each item. Nothing changes until you accept.

Permission: `draft`

```sh
mazemail propose [OPTIONS] --file <FILE>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--file <FILE>` | JSON file with the actions (an array, or `{ "title": …, "actions": [...] }`), `-` for stdin. Required. |
| `--title <TITLE>` | Title shown above the proposal in Maze. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  items: integer,  // Number of items.
  proposal: string,  // The proposal's id.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Propose actions from a file
mazemail propose --title 'Inbox triage' --file actions.json
```

### `mazemail rules`

List message rules (filters).

Lists the rules in the order they run, with their conditions, actions and whether they are enabled. Rules imported from Thunderbird start disabled; `problems` says what Maze can't run.

Permission: `read`

```sh
mazemail rules [OPTIONS]
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--account <ACCOUNT>` | Only this account's rules (and the rules for every account). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
[
  {
    account?: string | null,  // The account (`a_…`), or `null` for every account.
    actions: string,  // The actions, readable, in the order they run.
    conditions: string,  // The conditions, readable (`from contains "anna" and has attachments`).
    enabled: boolean,  // Whether it runs on new mail.
    id: string,  // Rule id (`r_…`).
    imported: boolean,  // Imported from Thunderbird.
    name: string,  // Name.
    note?: string | null,  // The import's note, if any.
    problems: string[],  // What keeps it from running as intended; unsupported actions are only skipped.
    runnable: boolean,  // Whether it may be enabled.
    trigger: string,  // When it runs: `arrival` or `arrival_before_junk` on new mail; others only with `rule run`.
  }
]
```

Examples:

```sh
# Show every rule
mazemail rules
# Imported rules that can be enabled
mazemail rules --json | jq -r '.[] | select(.imported and .runnable and (.enabled | not)) | .id'
```

### `mazemail rule show`

Show one rule.

Shows a rule's conditions, actions, problems and, for an imported rule, the import's note.

Permission: `read`

```sh
mazemail rule show <RULE>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<RULE>` | The rule's id (`r_…`, from `mazemail rules`). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  account?: string | null,  // The account (`a_…`), or `null` for every account.
  actions: string,  // The actions, readable, in the order they run.
  conditions: string,  // The conditions, readable (`from contains "anna" and has attachments`).
  enabled: boolean,  // Whether it runs on new mail.
  id: string,  // Rule id (`r_…`).
  imported: boolean,  // Imported from Thunderbird.
  name: string,  // Name.
  note?: string | null,  // The import's note, if any.
  problems: string[],  // What keeps it from running as intended; unsupported actions are only skipped.
  runnable: boolean,  // Whether it may be enabled.
  trigger: string,  // When it runs: `arrival` or `arrival_before_junk` on new mail; others only with `rule run`.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Show a rule
mazemail rule show r_b
```

### `mazemail rule add`

Add a rule for new mail.

Adds an enabled rule that runs on new mail in the Inbox, after the existing rules. --when takes from:, to:, cc:, subject:, list:, has:attachment and free text (the message text), negated with `-`; terms must all match, or any with OR. Always asks for approval in Maze.

Permission: `ask`

```sh
mazemail rule add [OPTIONS] --name <NAME> --when <QUERY> --then <ACTION>...
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--name <NAME>` | Name shown in the rules list. Required. |
| `--when <QUERY>` | Which messages the rule matches, as a search query: from:, to:, cc:, subject:, list:, has:attachment and free text (the message text), negated with `-`, all of them (`a b`) or any (`a OR b`). Required. |
| `--then <ACTION>...` | What to do (repeatable): archive, read, star, trash, stop, move:&lt;folder-id&gt;, copy:&lt;folder-id&gt;, tag:&lt;name&gt;. Required. |
| `--account <ACCOUNT>` | Limit the rule to one account. |
| `--apply-now` | Also run the rule on the messages in the Inbox now. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  id: string,  // Rule id.
  name: string,  // Name.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Tag and archive receipts
mazemail rule add --name Receipts --when 'from:receipts@shop.example' --then tag:Receipts --then archive
```

### `mazemail rule enable`

Enable a rule.

The rule runs on new mail from now on (mail that arrived before isn't filtered; use `mazemail rule run` for that). A rule with conditions Maze can't evaluate can't be enabled. Always asks for approval in Maze.

Permission: `ask`

```sh
mazemail rule enable <RULE>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<RULE>` | The rule's id (`r_…`, from `mazemail rules`). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  account?: string | null,  // The account (`a_…`), or `null` for every account.
  actions: string,  // The actions, readable, in the order they run.
  conditions: string,  // The conditions, readable (`from contains "anna" and has attachments`).
  enabled: boolean,  // Whether it runs on new mail.
  id: string,  // Rule id (`r_…`).
  imported: boolean,  // Imported from Thunderbird.
  name: string,  // Name.
  note?: string | null,  // The import's note, if any.
  problems: string[],  // What keeps it from running as intended; unsupported actions are only skipped.
  runnable: boolean,  // Whether it may be enabled.
  trigger: string,  // When it runs: `arrival` or `arrival_before_junk` on new mail; others only with `rule run`.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Enable an imported rule
mazemail rule enable r_b
```

### `mazemail rule disable`

Disable a rule.

The rule stops running on new mail; it stays in the list. Always asks for approval in Maze.

Permission: `ask`

```sh
mazemail rule disable <RULE>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<RULE>` | The rule's id (`r_…`, from `mazemail rules`). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  account?: string | null,  // The account (`a_…`), or `null` for every account.
  actions: string,  // The actions, readable, in the order they run.
  conditions: string,  // The conditions, readable (`from contains "anna" and has attachments`).
  enabled: boolean,  // Whether it runs on new mail.
  id: string,  // Rule id (`r_…`).
  imported: boolean,  // Imported from Thunderbird.
  name: string,  // Name.
  note?: string | null,  // The import's note, if any.
  problems: string[],  // What keeps it from running as intended; unsupported actions are only skipped.
  runnable: boolean,  // Whether it may be enabled.
  trigger: string,  // When it runs: `arrival` or `arrival_before_junk` on new mail; others only with `rule run`.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Disable a rule
mazemail rule disable r_b
```

### `mazemail rule delete`

Delete a rule.

Removes the rule; mail it already sorted stays where it is. Always asks for approval in Maze.

Permission: `ask`

```sh
mazemail rule delete <RULE>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<RULE>` | The rule's id (`r_…`, from `mazemail rules`). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  id: string,  // The deleted rule's id.
  name: string,  // Its name.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Delete a rule
mazemail rule delete r_b
```

### `mazemail rule run`

Run rules on a folder now.

Runs the enabled rules of the folder's account over every message in the folder, or only the rule you name (even a disabled one, to try it first). Actions are logged in the audit log with the rule as their origin. Always asks for approval in Maze.

Permission: `ask`

```sh
mazemail rule run --folder <FOLDER> [<RULE>]
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--folder <FOLDER>` | The folder to run the rules on (`f_…`, from `mazemail folders`). Required. |
| `<RULE>` | Only this rule (`r_…`), even if it is disabled (to try it). Default: the enabled rules of the folder's account. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  actions: integer,  // Actions run (each covers all its messages).
  affected: string[],  // Message ids changed.
  examined: integer,  // Messages the rules looked at.
  failed: integer,  // Actions skipped or failed (the audit log says why).
  matched: integer,  // Messages a rule acted on.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Run the enabled rules on a folder
mazemail rule run --folder f_k2
# Try one rule on a folder
mazemail rule run --folder f_k2 r_b
```

### `mazemail send`

Send a draft.

Disabled by default. A token approved with --allow-send may use it, and then every send waits for your approval in Maze ("waiting for approval in Maze…") and is never approved automatically.

Permission: `disabled`

```sh
mazemail send <DRAFT>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<DRAFT>` | The draft's id (`d_…`). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  draft: string,  // The sent draft.
  outbox: string,  // Outbox item id (sending happens in the background).
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Send after approval
mazemail send d_42
```

### `mazemail export`

Export messages to mbox or Maildir.

Writes the matching messages as plaintext to --to. Always asks for approval in Maze, because the export leaves encrypted storage.

Permission: `ask`

```sh
mazemail export [OPTIONS] --to <PATH> <QUERY>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<QUERY>` | Which messages, as a search query (`in:inbox`, `tag:receipts after:2026-01-01`). |
| `--to <PATH>` | Where to write (relative to the current directory). The export is plaintext. Required. |
| `--format <FORMAT>` | Format. One of `mbox`, `maildir`. Default: `mbox`. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  messages: integer,  // Messages exported.
  path: string,  // Where the export was written.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Export this year's inbox
mazemail export 'in:inbox after:2026-01-01' --to inbox.mbox
```

### `mazemail import`

Import an mbox file, a Maildir or a Thunderbird profile.

Uploads mbox and Maildir mail into a folder of an IMAP account (--account, --folder; the folder is created if missing), queued and sent by the sync engine; it shows up after the folder's next sync. Messages the folder already has are skipped. A Thunderbird profile brings its IMAP accounts, identities, tags, filters, saved searches and address books; POP3 accounts and Local Folders are skipped (Maze is IMAP only). --only picks parts of the profile (accounts, contacts, filters, saved-searches, tags); filters go to accounts an earlier import of the profile created and are skipped without them. Always asks for approval in Maze, which shows what is imported.

Permission: `ask`

```sh
mazemail import [OPTIONS] <PATH>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<PATH>` | File or directory to import (relative to the current directory). |
| `--format <FORMAT>` | Format (detected when omitted). One of `mbox`, `maildir`, `thunderbird`. |
| `--account <ACCOUNT>` | Account to upload mbox and Maildir mail into (may be left out when there is only one account). Not for Thunderbird profiles, which bring their own accounts. |
| `--folder <PATH>` | Folder of the account to upload into, as a path (`Archive/2019`); created on the server if missing. Default: a top-level folder named after the file. |
| `--only <PARTS>...` | Import only these parts of a Thunderbird profile, comma-separated (default: everything). Filters go to accounts already in Maze. One of `accounts`, `contacts`, `filters`, `saved-searches`, `tags`. Repeatable. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  accounts: integer,  // Accounts created (Thunderbird profiles).
  duplicates: integer,  // Messages skipped because the folder already has them or they are queued.
  folders: integer,  // Folders created.
  messages: integer,  // Messages queued for upload to the server (mbox, Maildir).
  skipped: string[],  // What wasn't imported and why ("POP3 account x@y skipped: …").
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Upload an mbox file into a folder of your account
mazemail import ~/old-mail.mbox --account a_b --folder Archive/2019
# Import a Thunderbird profile
mazemail import ~/Library/Thunderbird/Profiles/abcd.default-release
# Import only the address books and filters of a profile
mazemail import ~/.thunderbird/abcd.default-release --only contacts,filters
```

### `mazemail accounts`

List mail accounts and their sync state.

Lists the accounts in your token's scope with their server, sign-in method and sync state (up to date, syncing, offline, sign-in required).

Permission: `read`

```sh
mazemail accounts
```

Output (`--json`):

```ts
[
  {
    auth?: string | null,  // `password`, `oauth:google` or `oauth:microsoft`, for synced accounts.
    email?: string | null,  // The address, if known.
    id: string,  // Account id.
    kind: string,  // `imap`, `gmail`, `m365` or `local`.
    name: string,  // The name in the folder pane.
    server?: string | null,  // The IMAP server (`host:port`), for synced accounts.
    state: string,  // `connecting`, `idle`, `syncing`, `offline`, `auth_required`, `error` or `not_synced`.
    status: string,  // The sync state as a sentence.
  }
]
```

Examples:

```sh
# Accounts that need a new sign-in
mazemail accounts --json | jq -r '.[] | select(.state == "auth_required") | .id'
```

### `mazemail account add`

Add an IMAP, Gmail or Microsoft account.

Maze connects, logs in and lists the folders before it saves anything, then keeps the password (or the sign-in) in its encrypted storage and starts syncing. The password is read from the terminal without echo, or from stdin with --password-stdin; it is never a command-line argument. With --oauth google or --oauth microsoft, Maze opens the browser for the sign-in instead (Google needs auth.google.client_id in settings.json). Microsoft sign-ins use the auth.microsoft.tenant setting (default common); --tenant overrides it for this account, e.g. with your organization's tenant ID or domain when its app registration only allows its own accounts. The IMAP host, port and security are detected for well-known providers; so is the SMTP server for sending (override it with --smtp-host, --smtp-port and --smtp-security; it logs in like IMAP). Needs a write token, and you approve every account in Maze.

Permission: `admin`

```sh
mazemail account add [OPTIONS] <EMAIL>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<EMAIL>` | The account's address. |
| `--name <NAME>` | The name in the folder pane (default: the address). |
| `--oauth <OAUTH>` | Sign in with Google or Microsoft in the browser (no password). One of `google`, `microsoft`. |
| `--tenant <TENANT>` | With `--oauth microsoft`: the organization's tenant ID or domain, or `common`, `organizations`, `consumers` (default: the `auth.microsoft.tenant` setting). |
| `--imap-host <IMAP_HOST>` | IMAP host (default: detected from the address). |
| `--imap-port <IMAP_PORT>` | IMAP port (default: 993 for TLS, 143 otherwise). |
| `--imap-security <IMAP_SECURITY>` | IMAP transport security. One of `tls`, `starttls`, `none`. |
| `--username <USERNAME>` | Login name (default: the address). |
| `--password-stdin` | Read the password from stdin (first line) instead of prompting on the terminal. |
| `--smtp-host <SMTP_HOST>` | SMTP host for sending (default: derived from the address and the IMAP host). |
| `--smtp-port <SMTP_PORT>` | SMTP port (default: 465 for TLS, 587 for STARTTLS). |
| `--smtp-security <SMTP_SECURITY>` | SMTP transport security. One of `tls`, `starttls`, `none`. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  auth?: string | null,  // `password`, `oauth:google` or `oauth:microsoft`, for synced accounts.
  email?: string | null,  // The address, if known.
  id: string,  // Account id.
  kind: string,  // `imap`, `gmail`, `m365` or `local`.
  name: string,  // The name in the folder pane.
  server?: string | null,  // The IMAP server (`host:port`), for synced accounts.
  state: string,  // `connecting`, `idle`, `syncing`, `offline`, `auth_required`, `error` or `not_synced`.
  status: string,  // The sync state as a sentence.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Add an account; asks for the password
mazemail account add alice@example.org --imap-host imap.example.org
# Password from stdin (scripts)
printf '%s\n' "$PASSWORD" | mazemail account add alice@example.org --password-stdin
# Sign in with Google in the browser
mazemail account add bob@gmail.com --oauth google
# Sign in with Microsoft, for one organization (tenant)
mazemail account add dana@contoso.com --oauth microsoft --tenant contoso.com
```

### `mazemail account remove`

Remove a mail account.

Stops syncing the account and deletes its local mail, folders, identities and saved password or sign-in. Nothing changes on the server. Needs a write token, and you approve it in Maze.

Permission: `admin`

```sh
mazemail account remove <ACCOUNT>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<ACCOUNT>` | The account (`a_…`). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  id: string,  // The removed account's id.
  removed: boolean,  // Always `true`.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Remove an account
mazemail account remove a_b
```

### `mazemail account set`

Change an account's name, password or servers.

Changes what you pass and keeps the rest. A new --name moves the account's settings in settings.json (accounts.<name>, favorite folders) along with it. --password asks for a new password on the terminal without echo (--password-stdin reads it from stdin); it is never a command-line argument. Changing the IMAP server (--imap-host, --imap-port, --imap-security), the login name (--username) or the SMTP server (--smtp-host, --smtp-port, --smtp-security) asks for the password again: Maze only sends a saved password to the servers it was saved for. Like adding, Maze connects, logs in and lists the folders before it saves anything, then restarts the account's sync. Google and Microsoft accounts sign in again in the browser with --sign-in (--tenant changes a Microsoft account's tenant). Needs a write token, and you approve it in Maze.

Permission: `admin`

```sh
mazemail account set [OPTIONS] <ACCOUNT>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<ACCOUNT>` | The account (`a_…`, see `mazemail accounts`). |
| `--name <NAME>` | A new name in the folder pane. |
| `--imap-host <IMAP_HOST>` | IMAP host. |
| `--imap-port <IMAP_PORT>` | IMAP port. |
| `--imap-security <IMAP_SECURITY>` | IMAP transport security. One of `tls`, `starttls`, `none`. |
| `--username <USERNAME>` | Login name ("" for the address). |
| `--password` | Set a new password: asks for it on the terminal (without echo). Server changes ask for it anyway. |
| `--password-stdin` | Read the password from stdin (first line) instead of asking on the terminal. |
| `--smtp-host <SMTP_HOST>` | SMTP host for sending. |
| `--smtp-port <SMTP_PORT>` | SMTP port. |
| `--smtp-security <SMTP_SECURITY>` | SMTP transport security. One of `tls`, `starttls`, `none`. |
| `--sign-in` | Sign in with Google or Microsoft again, in the browser. |
| `--tenant <TENANT>` | With `--sign-in` on a Microsoft account: the tenant ID or domain, or `common`, `organizations`, `consumers` (default: the one it signed in with). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  auth?: string | null,  // `password`, `oauth:google` or `oauth:microsoft`, for synced accounts.
  email?: string | null,  // The address, if known.
  id: string,  // Account id.
  kind: string,  // `imap`, `gmail`, `m365` or `local`.
  name: string,  // The name in the folder pane.
  server?: string | null,  // The IMAP server (`host:port`), for synced accounts.
  state: string,  // `connecting`, `idle`, `syncing`, `offline`, `auth_required`, `error` or `not_synced`.
  status: string,  // The sync state as a sentence.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# The password changed: enter the new one
mazemail account set a_b --password
# Rename an account (its settings move along)
mazemail account set a_b --name Work
# Move to other servers (asks for the password)
mazemail account set a_b --imap-host mail.example.org --smtp-host mail.example.org
# Sign a Google or Microsoft account in again
mazemail account set a_c --sign-in
```

### `mazemail sync`

Sync accounts now.

Asks every account in your token's scope (or --account) to look for new mail and replay pending changes now, and retries at once when an account is offline. Returns right away; `mazemail accounts` shows the progress.

Permission: `read`

```sh
mazemail sync [OPTIONS]
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `--account <ACCOUNT>` | Only this account (default: every account in scope). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  accounts: string[],  // The accounts asked to sync now.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Sync every account
mazemail sync
# Sync one account
mazemail sync --account a_b
```

### `mazemail saved-searches`

List saved searches.

Lists the saved searches that Maze's folder pane shows under Saved Searches, with their queries. `mazemail search --saved <name>` runs one.

Permission: `read`

```sh
mazemail saved-searches
```

Output (`--json`):

```ts
[
  {
    id: string,  // Saved search id (`s_…`).
    name: string,  // Its name in the folder pane.
    query: string,  // Its query.
  }
]
```

Examples:

```sh
# Names of the saved searches
mazemail saved-searches --json | jq -r '.[].name'
```

### `mazemail saved-search add`

Save a search under a name.

Adds a saved search: Maze's folder pane shows it under Saved Searches, and selecting it lists the mail that matches the query, kept up to date as mail arrives. Names are unique (ignoring case). Nothing about your mail changes.

Permission: `draft`

```sh
mazemail saved-search add <NAME> <QUERY>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<NAME>` | The name the folder pane shows (unique, ignoring case). |
| `<QUERY>` | The search query, in `mazemail search`'s language. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  id: string,  // Saved search id (`s_…`).
  name: string,  // Its name in the folder pane.
  query: string,  // Its query.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Save a search for invoices
mazemail saved-search add Invoices 'invoice has:attachment'
```

### `mazemail saved-search rename`

Rename a saved search.

Gives a saved search (named by its name or id) a new name.

Permission: `draft`

```sh
mazemail saved-search rename <SAVED_SEARCH> <NEW_NAME>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<SAVED_SEARCH>` | The saved search: its name or id (`s_…`). |
| `<NEW_NAME>` | The new name. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  id: string,  // Saved search id (`s_…`).
  name: string,  // Its name in the folder pane.
  query: string,  // Its query.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Rename a saved search
mazemail saved-search rename Invoices 'Bills'
```

### `mazemail saved-search edit`

Change the query of a saved search.

Replaces the query of a saved search (named by its name or id). Its folder pane entry shows the new results at once.

Permission: `draft`

```sh
mazemail saved-search edit <SAVED_SEARCH> <QUERY>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<SAVED_SEARCH>` | The saved search: its name or id (`s_…`). |
| `<QUERY>` | The new query. |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  id: string,  // Saved search id (`s_…`).
  name: string,  // Its name in the folder pane.
  query: string,  // Its query.
}
```

Examples:

```sh
# Widen a saved search
mazemail saved-search edit Invoices 'invoice OR receipt has:attachment'
```

### `mazemail saved-search delete`

Delete a saved search.

Removes a saved search (named by its name or id) from the folder pane. The mail it found stays where it is. Always asks for approval in Maze.

Permission: `ask`

```sh
mazemail saved-search delete <SAVED_SEARCH>
```

| Argument | Description |
| -------- | ----------- |
| `<SAVED_SEARCH>` | The saved search: its name or id (`s_…`). |

Output (`--json`):

```ts
{
  deleted: boolean,  // Always `true`.
